page("sausage-and-peppers-compact-grill","Sausage and Peppers on a Compact Charcoal Grill","Italian sausage or brats with peppers and onions on the Slot Grill Compact: gentle cooking so casings don't burst, griddle vegetables, and hoagie assembly.",IMG["both16"],recipe2(
"<p>Sausage and peppers is the ultimate tailgate and camp food: cheap, easy to pack, forgiving, and it holds well in a covered pan while you cook the next round. On a small grill the trick is patience. Sausages are full of fat; blast them over hot coals and the casings split, fat pours onto the fire and you get flare-ups and dry links. Cook them on the cooler side of a two-zone fire while the peppers and onions soften on a griddle.</p><blockquote>\"Good quality, perfect for on the go grilling, and great for job sites for the blue collar guys out there.\"<cite>Verified Compact owner</cite></blockquote>",
"3–4","About 35–40 minutes",
["5–6 raw Italian sausages or bratwurst links","2 bell peppers (mixed colors), sliced into strips","1 large onion, sliced","2 tablespoons olive oil","Salt, pepper, pinch of oregano or chili flakes","Hoagie rolls or buns","Mustard or marinara (optional)"],
["<b>Light a fuller chimney</b> and build a two-zone fire. Put a <a href='"+GRID+"'>griddle</a> over the medium part and the grate over the edge of the hot zone and the cool zone.","<b>Start the vegetables</b> on the oiled griddle with salt. Stir occasionally; they take 15–20 minutes to soften and brown.","<b>Place sausages on the cool side</b> of the grate. Don't prick them; you'll lose juice.","<b>Cook gently,</b> turning every few minutes, 15–20 minutes, until nearly done.","<b>Brown over the coals</b> for the last few minutes, turning to color all sides. Move any that flare.","<b>Check temperature:</b> raw pork sausages to 160°F (poultry sausages to 165°F).","<b>Toast rolls</b> on the grate briefly.","<b>Combine</b> sausages with peppers and onions in a foil pan to hold, or build sandwiches right away."],
why="<ul><li>The cool zone cooks fatty links gently without bursting.</li><li>The griddle handles sliced vegetables.</li><li>Everything holds in a covered pan at the cool end for waves of guests.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Beer brats:</b> simmer brats with onions and beer in a foil pan at the cool end after grilling to hold.","<b>Pre-cooked sausages</b> (smoked links): just heat through and brown, 8–10 minutes.","<b>Skewers:</b> sliced pre-cooked sausage with peppers, onion and shrimp.","<b>Breakfast:</b> leftover sausage and peppers on the griddle with eggs."],
trouble=[("Casings burst","Too hot; cook on the cool side first."),("Flare-ups","Move links to the cool side until flames die down."),("Peppers raw while sausage done","Start vegetables first; they take longer.")],
serve="<p>Hold finished sausages and vegetables in a covered foil pan at the cool end. Pour grease into a can once cooled. Refrigerate leftovers within 2 hours.</p>"),"recipes",
[("How long do sausages take on a charcoal grill?","Raw sausages usually take about 15 to 25 minutes over medium or indirect heat, then a few minutes over direct heat to brown. Cook raw pork sausage to 160°F."),("Should I poke holes in sausages before grilling?","No. Pricking lets juices and fat escape, causing dry sausages and flare-ups."),("How do I stop sausage casings from splitting?","Cook them gently on the cool side of a two-zone fire and only brown them over direct heat at the end."),("Can I cook peppers and onions on a grill?","Yes. Use a griddle plate so sliced vegetables don't fall through the grates.")])

page("grilled-vegetables-compact-grill","Grilled Vegetables on a Small Charcoal Grill","How to grill corn, zucchini, peppers, onions, mushrooms, asparagus, potatoes and more on the Slot Grill Compact. Cut sizes, timing chart and seasoning.",IMG["pl1"],recipe2(
"<p>Vegetables over charcoal get char, sweetness and smoke you can't get in a pan. On a small grill, vegetables are also a smart way to use the fire: cook them while meat rests, or make them the main event. The rules are simple: cut them so they have flat sides, oil them lightly, season well, and use the right zone for each.</p>",
"2–4 as a side","About 25–35 minutes",
["2 ears corn, husked","1 zucchini or yellow squash, sliced lengthwise ½\" thick","1 bell pepper, quartered","1 red onion, sliced into ½\" rings (keep rings together with a toothpick)","8 oz mushrooms (whole, on skewers) or 2 portobello caps","1 bunch asparagus, woody ends snapped off","2–3 tablespoons olive oil","Salt, pepper, garlic powder","Finish: lemon, butter, fresh herbs, a little vinegar"],
["<b>Prep.</b> Cut vegetables into large pieces with flat sides. Small pieces fall through; use the <a href='"+GRID+"'>griddle</a> or skewers for them.","<b>Oil and season</b> lightly. Too much oil drips and flares.","<b>Light a chimney</b> and build a two-zone fire.","<b>Cook in order of time,</b> using the chart below. Start dense vegetables first.","<b>Turn once or twice,</b> when you see good grill marks.","<b>Finish</b> with butter on corn, lemon and herbs on squash and asparagus, a splash of vinegar on peppers."],
extra="<h2>Vegetable timing chart</h2><div class='tbl'><table><tr><th>Vegetable</th><th>Cut</th><th>Heat</th><th>Approx. time</th></tr><tr><td>Corn</td><td>Whole ears, husked</td><td>Medium-high</td><td>10–15 min, turning</td></tr><tr><td>Zucchini / squash</td><td>½\" lengthwise planks</td><td>Medium-high</td><td>3–5 min per side</td></tr><tr><td>Bell peppers</td><td>Quarters</td><td>High</td><td>4–6 min per side</td></tr><tr><td>Onions</td><td>½\" rings, pinned</td><td>Medium</td><td>5–7 min per side</td></tr><tr><td>Asparagus</td><td>Whole spears, across grates</td><td>High</td><td>4–6 min, rolling</td></tr><tr><td>Portobello</td><td>Whole caps</td><td>Medium</td><td>4–6 min per side</td></tr><tr><td>Potatoes</td><td>Par-cooked, halved or thick slices</td><td>Medium</td><td>8–12 min, turning</td></tr><tr><td>Tomatoes</td><td>Halves</td><td>High</td><td>2–3 min</td></tr></table></div><p>Times are approximate. Cook until tender and charred where you want it.</p>",
why="<ul><li>A two-zone fire lets dense vegetables cook through while quick ones char fast.</li><li>The griddle catches small and sliced vegetables.</li><li>Vegetables fill the grill while meat rests.</li></ul>",
variations=["<b>Elote-style corn:</b> brush with mayo, chili powder, cheese and lime.","<b>Foil packets:</b> diced potatoes, onions, butter and seasoning in heavy foil, 20–30 minutes on medium, turning once.","<b>Vegetable skewers</b> with cherry tomatoes, onion and mushrooms.","<b>Grilled salad:</b> char romaine halves 1 minute cut-side down."],
trouble=[("Falling through","Bigger cuts, skewers or the griddle."),("Burnt outside, raw inside","Dense vegetables need medium heat or par-cooking."),("Mushy","Overcooked or sliced too thin.")],
serve="<p>Serve vegetables warm or at room temperature with a squeeze of lemon. Leftovers are great chopped into breakfast tacos or salads.</p>"),"recipes",
[("What vegetables are best on a charcoal grill?","Corn, zucchini, squash, peppers, onions, asparagus, mushrooms, tomatoes and par-cooked potatoes all grill well."),("How do I keep vegetables from falling through the grate?","Cut them into large pieces, use skewers, or cook them on a griddle plate."),("Should I oil vegetables before grilling?","Yes, lightly. Too much oil drips and causes flare-ups."),("Do I need to par-cook potatoes for the grill?","It helps. Par-cooked potatoes only need to brown and heat through.")])
